 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of small cells, involving sorting and texture map creation. Interactive rendering is $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of large cells plus the number of splat rectangles, leveraging hardware rasterization.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for texture maps, which scale with the number of slices and the size of the splat footprints.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Scaling:** Scales well with parallelization of the preprocessing steps (cell projection and sorting) [S1, S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents loss of detail in adaptive meshes; avoids the complexity of rendering filtered polygons; leverages hardware acceleration.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires preprocessing; relies on accurate ellipsoid fitting; may introduce artifacts if the Gaussian approximation is poor.
-*   **When to use:** When rendering adaptive meshes with small cells that might be missed by slice planes.
-*   **When not to use:** When cells are uniformly large and easily captured by slice planes.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing involves sorting and projecting all small cells, which is $O(N \log N)$ for sorting and $O(N)$ for projection, where $N$ is the number of small cells. Interactive rendering is efficient as splats are pre-computed into texture maps.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing texture maps for all slices. The size depends on the number of slices and the size of the bounding rectangles. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Prevents loss of detail in adaptive meshes; handles small cells that would otherwise be missed; efficient interactive rendering via texture mapping.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires preprocessing; complex to implement due to the need for ellipsoid fitting and stencil buffer management; relies on hardware texture mapping capabilities.
+*   **When to use:** When rendering adaptive meshes with significant variation in cell size, and small cells contain important geometric details.
+*   **When not to use:** When cells are uniformly large, or when pre-processing time is prohibitive.
 
 ## Background
-This method was developed by Silva et al. and presented in the paper "Volume rendering for curvilinear and unstructured grids" [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of standard slicing techniques for unstructured grids, particularly in the context of adaptive meshes where small cells contain critical information [S1, S2][S3, unofficial]. The approach builds on EWA splatting principles [S5, S10, abstract only, unofficial] to provide anti-aliasing for small geometric primitives.
+This method was developed for volume rendering on curvilinear and unstructured grids, specifically to address the issue of small cells in adaptive meshes being missed during slice-based rendering [S1][S3, unofficial]. It builds on the work of Williams et al. [8] regarding spine splats and Zwicker et al. [5] regarding Gaussian presampling filters [S1][S3, unofficial]. The technique was presented in the context of interactive visualization of large datasets, requiring parallelization for performance [S1][S3, unofficial].
 
